    clinical:
      "<b>Why this matters at the clinic and the post-mortem table.</b><br>" +
      "<b>A bad sample is worse than no sample</b>, because it produces a number that looks authoritative and is wrong. The commonest and most dangerous example is <b>haemolysis causing spurious hyperkalaemia</b> — a result that has led to animals being treated for a potassium disorder they never had. If a sample is haemolysed, say so on the form and repeat it rather than reporting the potassium.<br><br>" +
      "Two collection habits prevent most problems. First, <b>fill the tube to the mark and invert it gently eight to ten times</b>. An underfilled EDTA tube gives a falsely low PCV, and an underfilled citrate tube gives falsely prolonged clotting times — both errors point the clinician in exactly the wrong direction. Second, <b>make the blood smear at the animal's side</b>, before you drive back to the clinic. EDTA distorts cells within a couple of hours, and a smear made from a sample that travelled all afternoon will show artefactual changes that can be mistaken for disease.<br><br>" +
      "<b>Match the tube to the test</b>, and remember the traps. Lavender EDTA for the haemogram; green heparin or a plain tube for biochemistry; blue citrate at exactly 9:1 for clotting times; grey fluoride whenever glucose matters — which includes every suspected ketosis case in a dairy cow and every diabetic dog whose sample will not reach the laboratory quickly. Never send an EDTA sample and then ask for a serum calcium; the anticoagulant has already removed the calcium you wanted to measure.<br><br>" +
      "Finally, interpret results against the <b>animal's physiological state</b>. A fractious cat restrained forcibly will show a leukocytosis that means nothing; an excited horse will show a PCV several points higher than its true value; a dog on prednisolone will show a stress leukogram and a high alkaline phosphatase. Recording the circumstances of collection on the form is not bureaucracy — it is what allows the result to be read correctly."
    ,

